  "summary": "Traditional architecture of the Lanna Kingdom characterized by wooden structures, elevated bases, and decorative elements reflecting Lanna cultural identity.",
  "description": "Lanna architecture features teak wood construction, elevated platforms to prevent flooding, and intricate carvings of mythical creatures reflecting local beliefs and craftsmanship. Its design represents the distinct artistic traditions of Northern Thailand, influenced by Burmese and Mon aesthetics.",
  "culturalContext": "Lanna architecture — the northern Thai (Chiang Mai, Chiang Rai) kingdom's building tradition — elevates teak temples (wat) on sling-stone bases: huge carved ho trai scripture libraries on high stilted platforms, multi-tiered thatch-and-teak roofs, chofa-less finials of naga serpents, and viharn halls whose carved gables (phan fa) blaze gold. Burmese and Shan craft enriched 19th-century rebuilds (Wat Phra That Doi Suthep, Wat Chedi Luang); Lanna's warm timber glow now defines \"northern Thai\" resort architecture worldwide.",
